    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124439,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 23,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>, above.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:35:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124440,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 23,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "<p>Well-circumscribed, variably sized nodules of basaloid cells with peripheral palisading and retraction spaces are seen; disorganized nuclear arrangement with apoptosis is frequently observed in the centre of the nodules. The tumour is usually connected with the epidermis. The stroma is paucicellular; amyloid deposition or keloidal-type collagenous stroma may be seen, but not desmoplasia. Functional melanocytes are found in many BCCs and may be associated with melanin pigment in tumour nests and in stromal melanophages {27612950}.</p>\r\n<p>Patterns of nodular BCC include pigmented, keratotic, cystic, and nodulocystic. Keratinizing BCC tumour nests show central mature keratinization. Nodulocystic or cystic BCC shows cystic degeneration within tumour islands. Cribriform tumour nests are seen in adenoid BCC. Less common patterns include rippled, labyrinthine, or trabecular arrays. Clear, signet-ring, granular, and giant (monster) cell types have been described {8227606; 226002; 1714249; 8468420}.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>Cases with small nodules should be differentiated from aggressive micronodular BCC. Distinction from benign and malignant trichoblastic tumours, especially trichoblastoma, can be difficult in some cases. The presence of papillary mesenchymal bodies and eosinophilic fibroblastic stroma encasing tumour nodules but retracting from the surrounding dermis favours the latter. Lesions with cystic change may simulate other adnexal tumours, particularly adenoid cystic carcinoma. BerEP4 expression is found in BCC and Merkel cell carcinoma, which may cause diagnostic difficulties. Conversely, BCC can express markers of neuroendocrine differentiation, such as chromogranin and synaptophysin in as many as 27% of cases {23292839}, but it lacks the dot-like perinuclear positivity for CK20 typical of Merkel cell carcinoma.</p>\r\n<p>Ulcerated BCC may show superficial mature-appearing squamoid change immediately beneath the ulcer, raising the possibility of squamous cell carcinoma. Basosquamous carcinoma shows malignant-appearing squamous elements admixed with typical basaloid carcinoma elements {32166375}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:35:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
